<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilyas Shahi dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1342">1342</a>–<a href="/years/1414">1414</a>; <a href="/years/1435">1435</a>–<a href="/years/1487">1487</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ilyas Shahi dynasty was the first royal house of the united Bengal Sultanate. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamsuddin-ilyas-shah">Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah</a>, brought all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> under one ruler by 1352 and resisted an invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. The family ruled from Pandua and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a>, lost the throne to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ganesha">Raja Ganesha</a> around 1414, was restored in 1435 and was overthrown by Abyssinian palace guards in 1487.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ilyas-shahi-dynasty.jpg" alt="Ilyas Shahi dynasty" width="320" height="297" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Beylarbey · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Ilyas_Shahis%E2%80%93Ganesha.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ancestors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamsuddin-ilyas-shah">Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah</a> came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>; according to Syed A M R Haque they arrived in the subcontinent as Muslim missionaries and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagirs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1227, when it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Ilyas Shah took service under Izz al-Din Yahya, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satgaon">Satgaon</a>, and after Yahya's death in 1338 seized Satgaon and declared himself a sultan independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. He went on to defeat Alauddin Ali Shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Lakhnauti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikhtiyaruddin-ghazi-shah">Ikhtiyaruddin Ghazi Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonargaon">Sonargaon</a>, making Bengal a single political entity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-shah-bengal">Sikandar Shah</a> ruled for some 30 years and built the Adina Masjid at Pandua in 1368 and the Kotwali Darwaza at Gauḍa. Sikandar's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyasuddin-azam-shah">Ghiyasuddin Azam Shah</a> established friendly relations with Ming China and encouraged trade, and in his reign the Chinese traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-huan">Ma Huan</a> visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p>Political confusion and weakness then let the family of the slave Shihabuddin Bayazid Shah overthrow Saifuddin Hamza Shah, and the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> soon followed. Ganesha's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalaluddin-muhammad-shah">Jadu</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and reigned as Jalal ad-Din Muhammad Shah, and was succeeded by his son Shamsuddin Ahmad Shah, whom his nobles killed in an attempt to restore the old line. Two short-lived rulers, Qutbuddin Azam Shah and Ghiyasuddin Nusrat Shah, struck coins in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in AH 837 (1434 CE), and a third, Siraj-al Din Sikandar Shah, is known only from undated coins; whether any was related to Ahmad is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The dynasty was restored by Mahmud Shah, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamsuddin-ilyas-shah">Shamsuddin Ilyas Shah</a>, who took the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasiruddin-mahmud-shah">Nasiruddin Mahmud Shah</a>. Its last ruler, Jalal ad-Din Fatih Shah, was killed in 1487 by Shahzada Barbak, the Habshi commander of the palace guards, who became sultan as Barbak Shah. The dynasty laid the foundations of the late medieval Sunni Muslim Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ilyas Shahi dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Ilyas Shahi dynasty</b> governed from Pandua, and later the old city of Gaur again became the capital, with the delta's rivers serving as both highways and defences.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
